0.05 0.50 0.55 RG 1.20 w 50.0 612.0 m 545.0 612.0 l S
0.09 0.18 0.40 rg BT /FB 9.0 Tf 50.0 610.0 Td (2.2  Why Logit-Based Distillation is Not Applicable Here) Tj ET
0 0 0 rg
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 596.0 Td (Logit-based distillation \(Hinton et al., 2015\) minimises KL-divergence between the student's and teacher's) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 584.5 Td (full output distributions over their shared vocabulary. This requires identical tokenisers and vocabulary) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 573.0 Td (sizes. phi3.5 uses a SentencePiece BPE tokeniser with vocabulary size 32,064, while pythia-70m uses) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 561.5 Td (GPT-NeoX byte-level BPE with vocabulary size 50,304. Because token indices map to entirely different) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 550.0 Td (subword units, aligning logit vectors is undefined without introducing a learned cross-vocabulary) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 538.5 Td (projection layer - an engineering complexity that is out of scope for a 70M CPU-only student. Additionally,) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 527.0 Td (logit distillation requires running the teacher in inference mode with the student's partial generations at) Tj ET
BT /FR 8.5 Tf 50.0 515.5 Td (each step, which is computationally infeasible when the teacher is served over an HTTP API.) Tj ET
